WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.680 --> 00:00:05.120
I'm going to go out on a limb here&nbsp;
and guess that you've probably heard&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.120 --> 00:00:10.320
of the Knights Templar. Despite apparently&nbsp;
having apparently been disbanded&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:10.320 --> 00:00:16.320
getting on for a millennium ago, this&nbsp;
secretive order of Christian warrior monks&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:16.320 --> 00:00:19.760
remains surprisingly prominent&nbsp;
in the public consciousness,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:19.760 --> 00:00:26.400
cropping up in video games, TV shows,&nbsp;
novels, and films with impressive regularity.

00:00:27.280 --> 00:00:32.800
Perhaps that has something to do with the&nbsp;
widespread rumours that the Knights Templar&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:32.800 --> 00:00:38.560
never really went away, and that the remnants of&nbsp;
this secret order continue to pull the strings of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:38.560 --> 00:00:44.720
society from the shadows even today. Whatever&nbsp;
the reason, it's clear the Knights Templar&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:44.720 --> 00:00:50.960
have gotten themselves tangled up with a huge&nbsp;
range of myths and legends over the centuries,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:50.960 --> 00:00:55.760
from the holy grail to the ark of the&nbsp;
covenant and the founding of the freemasons.

00:00:56.320 --> 00:01:01.440
But as exciting as all these conspiracy&nbsp;
theories are, this is one of those rare&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:01.440 --> 00:01:07.760
cases where the true story is every bit as&nbsp;
interesting as all the probably-made-up ones.

00:01:07.760 --> 00:01:14.640
Because, from incredibly humble beginnings&nbsp;
and in a startlingly short space of time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:14.640 --> 00:01:20.960
the real Knights Templar rose to become one of&nbsp;
the most influential organisations in history.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:22.720 --> 00:01:28.800
During their meteoric rise, the Templars&nbsp;
attained unimaginable wealth, broke new&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:28.800 --> 00:01:35.200
boundaries in the business world, and wielded&nbsp;
the kind of power that makes even kings jealous.

00:01:35.920 --> 00:01:42.400
But, as is often the case with rapid rises, the&nbsp;
fall of the Knights Templar was, if anything,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:42.400 --> 00:01:48.800
even more spectacular, ending with one of the&nbsp;
most powerful men on earth being burnt at the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:48.800 --> 00:01:55.280
stake in the Shadow of Notre Dame cathedral while&nbsp;
spitting curses like Kanye West on a dis track.

00:01:56.080 --> 00:01:58.480
But... I'm getting ahead of myself.

00:01:59.200 --> 00:02:03.280
To best tell the story of the Knights&nbsp;
Templar, I should probably start&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:03.280 --> 00:02:11.680
at the beginning - a beginning that can be found&nbsp;
almost a thousand years ago in 1119 AD, exactly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:11.680 --> 00:02:18.480
20 years after the Franks captured Jerusalem from&nbsp;
the Fatimid Caliphate during the First Crusade.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:19.200 --> 00:02:24.240
With Jerusalem suddenly under Christian&nbsp;
control, Christian pilgrims from across&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:24.240 --> 00:02:29.280
Europe began flocking to the Holy Land to&nbsp;
visit the many sacred sites found there.

00:02:30.080 --> 00:02:34.800
But these pilgrimages were dangerous.&nbsp;
While the city of Jerusalem itself&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:34.800 --> 00:02:39.760
was well guarded, much of the rest of&nbsp;
the Holy Land - which stretches from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:39.760 --> 00:02:45.680
the Mediterranean Sea to the banks of the&nbsp;
river Jordan - wasn't. Bandits were common,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:45.680 --> 00:02:51.120
and pilgrims were easy targets.&nbsp;
Thousands were robbed or even killed.

00:02:55.040 --> 00:03:01.040
Sickened by this wanton slaughter, a French&nbsp;
knight by the name of Hugues de Payens decided&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:01.040 --> 00:03:08.320
to transform himself into the hero the Holy&nbsp;
Land needed. So, he gathered together 8 of his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:08.320 --> 00:03:15.360
knightly mates, and, with the blessing of King&nbsp;
Baldwin II of Jerusalem, formed a new monastic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:15.360 --> 00:03:22.240
order with the sole aim of protecting Christian&nbsp;
pilgrims on their travels around the holy land.

00:03:22.800 --> 00:03:29.040
To begin with, that's all it was - a few brave&nbsp;
knights chaperoning pilgrims. Despite having been&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:29.040 --> 00:03:35.280
given shiny new headquarters on Temple Mount in&nbsp;
Jerusalem by the king himself, the knights were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:35.280 --> 00:03:42.160
incredibly poor, relying on the charity of those&nbsp;
they protected to survive. Their lack of funds&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:42.160 --> 00:03:47.040
(along with the location of the headquarters)&nbsp;
actually inspired their original name - 'The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:47.040 --> 00:03:52.480
Poor Knights of the Temple of King Solomon.' But&nbsp;
that was a bit of a mouthful, and so eventually&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:52.480 --> 00:04:00.160
it was shortened to the name that remains&nbsp;
so famous even today - the Knights Templar.

00:04:00.160 --> 00:04:05.840
This small band of warrior monks did&nbsp;
their protection job exceptionally well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:05.840 --> 00:04:11.440
and soon enough their reputation in Christendom&nbsp;
began to grow, helped in no small part by the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:11.440 --> 00:04:17.120
fact that one of the original knights had a nephew&nbsp;
who was going places in the catholic church. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:17.120 --> 00:04:24.720
when I say he was going places, he was eventually&nbsp;
made a saint - when he talked, Catholics listened.

00:04:24.720 --> 00:04:30.960
Before long, the Knights Templar were the&nbsp;
trendiest charity in town, and anyone who&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:30.960 --> 00:04:36.720
was anyone suddenly wanted to be seen to be&nbsp;
supporting them. Some donated cold hard cash,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:36.720 --> 00:04:43.680
others left land or valuables to the Templars in&nbsp;
their wills, and noble families started sending&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:43.680 --> 00:04:50.560
any spare male sons they had lying around to&nbsp;
join the order and make a name for themselves.

00:04:51.200 --> 00:04:57.920
By 1135 AD, even the Pope was feeling a bit&nbsp;
of FOMO, which he quenched by issuing the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:57.920 --> 00:05:04.560
Templars with a large monetary donation.&nbsp;
4 years later, he passed a Papal order&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:04.560 --> 00:05:12.240
that made the Templars exempt from paying taxes&nbsp;
and essentially put them outside of local law in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:12.240 --> 00:05:18.960
whichever countries they operated in - in short,&nbsp;
they were answerable only to the Pope himself.

00:05:22.000 --> 00:05:26.800
From there, the power and popularity&nbsp;
of the Templars continued to snowball.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:27.440 --> 00:05:29.920
At their peak, there were thought to be as many as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:30.880 --> 00:05:36.480
20,000 members spread across pretty much&nbsp;
the whole of Europe and the Middle East.

00:05:36.480 --> 00:05:40.560
But the Templars never let this success&nbsp;
go to their heads. The life of a knight&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:40.560 --> 00:05:47.360
was heavily regimented and restricted. All new&nbsp;
inductees were made to take vows of poverty,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:47.360 --> 00:05:54.480
chastity, piety, and obedience. They also&nbsp;
had to follow several hundred strict rules&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:54.480 --> 00:05:57.520
that dictated how they should&nbsp;
lead their day to day lives.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:58.160 --> 00:06:03.200
These rules were incredibly prescriptive,&nbsp;
outlining what the knights could eat,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:03.760 --> 00:06:09.760
how they should spend their free time, and&nbsp;
what they could wear. Most importantly of all,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:09.760 --> 00:06:15.360
the rules made it clear that the Knights were&nbsp;
to avoid fraternising with women at all costs.

00:06:16.320 --> 00:06:22.160
All that pent up sexual frustration seems to&nbsp;
have paid off, because the Knights had a truly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:22.160 --> 00:06:29.120
fearsome reputation on the battlefields of the&nbsp;
Holy Land. They excelled to such an extent that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:29.120 --> 00:06:36.720
they evolved from pilgrim protectors to an&nbsp;
elite corps of heavily armoured shock troops&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:36.720 --> 00:06:41.520
that were deployed wherever the fighting&nbsp;
was fiercest throughout the crusades.

00:06:42.320 --> 00:06:48.640
The knights were well trained and expensively&nbsp;
armed, but it was their bravery that really&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:48.640 --> 00:06:56.480
stood out - they were forbidden from retreating&nbsp;
in battle unless every single Templar flag on the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:56.480 --> 00:07:02.640
field had fallen. It helped that dying&nbsp;
in battle was seen as a great honour,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:02.640 --> 00:07:09.200
granting the fallen knight automatic queue jump&nbsp;
privileges at the gates of heaven. As a result,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:09.200 --> 00:07:15.840
the Templars showed no fear even&nbsp;
in the most hopeless situations.

00:07:16.480 --> 00:07:21.200
Away from the battlefields of the Holy Land,&nbsp;
things were going equally well for the Order,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:21.200 --> 00:07:26.960
which had become so rich so quickly it&nbsp;
hardly knew what to do with all the cash.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:26.960 --> 00:07:31.760
Eventually, the Templars settled&nbsp;
on basically doing everything.

00:07:31.760 --> 00:07:36.640
Real estate was one of the biggest focuses,&nbsp;
and as well as buying up huge swathes of land&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:36.640 --> 00:07:41.440
in Europe and the Middle East, they built&nbsp;
castles and cathedrals by the hundreds,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:41.440 --> 00:07:46.480
many of which still stand to this day.&nbsp;
They bought up vineyards, farmland,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:46.480 --> 00:07:51.680
and stables, got involved in manufacturing,&nbsp;
and hired blacksmiths, builders and squires.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:52.320 --> 00:07:58.800
They also bought a large fleet of ships, and,&nbsp;
most impressively of all, the entire island&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:58.800 --> 00:08:06.160
of Cyprus. Off the back of these achievements&nbsp;
and more, some modern historians consider&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:06.160 --> 00:08:11.840
the Knights Templar to have been the&nbsp;
world's first multinational corporation.

00:08:12.400 --> 00:08:16.880
One of the most successful aspects of&nbsp;
this business empire was dedicated to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:16.880 --> 00:08:22.880
what can be considered a prototype of the&nbsp;
modern concept of international banking.

00:08:23.680 --> 00:08:29.600
The system started out as an innovative way to&nbsp;
help Christians with the logistics of visiting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:29.600 --> 00:08:36.080
the Holy Land. A pilgrim could head to a Templar&nbsp;
house in their home country before setting off&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:36.720 --> 00:08:44.160
to hand over valuables or cash in return for&nbsp;
a letter of credit. That credit could then be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:44.160 --> 00:08:48.960
'withdrawn' from any other Templar&nbsp;
house anywhere else in the world.

00:08:49.520 --> 00:08:55.040
It was actually kind of ingenious. Not only did&nbsp;
it give the Templars another way to make money,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:55.040 --> 00:09:01.600
it also helped them with their original mission&nbsp;
of protecting Christians in the Holy Land. Since&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:01.600 --> 00:09:07.120
pilgrims no longer had to carry valuables&nbsp;
with them on their trip to Jerusalem,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:07.120 --> 00:09:10.000
they became far less attractive to bandits.

00:09:10.720 --> 00:09:16.000
Over time, the financial services offered by&nbsp;
the Templars expanded to include the pawning&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:16.000 --> 00:09:22.160
of valuables, money lending, and even the&nbsp;
paying of ransoms on credit. These services&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:22.160 --> 00:09:27.920
were so highly regarded they were used by some of&nbsp;
the most powerful people in Europe at the time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:27.920 --> 00:09:33.200
including at least two kings - King Henry III of&nbsp;
England is said to have pawned his Crown Jewels&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:33.200 --> 00:09:39.360
with the Templars in order to raise some quick&nbsp;
cash to help quell an uprising among his Barons,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:39.360 --> 00:09:44.480
and King Philip IV of France borrowed&nbsp;
significant sums from the Templars&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:44.480 --> 00:09:51.360
to finance a series of expensive wars, mostly&nbsp;
with the English (it was always the English).

00:09:51.920 --> 00:09:56.160
Somehow, in under 200 years,&nbsp;
what had started out as 9&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:56.160 --> 00:10:00.800
poverty-stricken knights protecting&nbsp;
pilgrims on the road to Jerusalem&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:00.800 --> 00:10:06.000
had somehow grown into a multinational&nbsp;
conglomerate with the power of a small country.

00:10:06.800 --> 00:10:13.840
The trouble was, while the Templars had the riches&nbsp;
and influence of a nation, they didn’t actually&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:13.840 --> 00:10:22.000
control one. And that meant their entire existence&nbsp;
relied on the goodwill of the men who did - the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:22.000 --> 00:10:28.800
kings of Europe. Unfortunately for the Templars,&nbsp;
that goodwill was about to abruptly run out.

00:10:31.280 --> 00:10:37.280
It started with the fall of Jerusalem to&nbsp;
legendary general Saladin and his armies in 1187.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:37.920 --> 00:10:44.000
The Christians did manage to regain the city again&nbsp;
in 1229, but they lost it again 15 years later.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:44.640 --> 00:10:49.440
Jerusalem would remain under Muslim&nbsp;
control for the next 700 years.

00:10:50.400 --> 00:10:55.040
The Templars had diversified admirably&nbsp;
in the years since their foundation,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:55.760 --> 00:11:00.640
but at their core, they existed to&nbsp;
fight for Christianity in the Holy Land.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:01.520 --> 00:11:06.800
With that land now under new ownership,&nbsp;
for the first time since their formation,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:07.440 --> 00:11:13.920
people began to question whether or not the&nbsp;
Knights Templar were really necessary anymore.

00:11:13.920 --> 00:11:17.680
By this point they should have been&nbsp;
big enough and powerful enough to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:17.680 --> 00:11:22.400
shrug that kind of criticism off.&nbsp;
And they probably would have done,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:22.400 --> 00:11:27.760
had their own success not come back to&nbsp;
bite them at the worst possible time.

00:11:27.760 --> 00:11:31.840
I mentioned a moment ago that Philip&nbsp;
IV of France had borrowed money from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:31.840 --> 00:11:38.240
the Templars to fight the English. What I didn't&nbsp;
mentioned is that he'd borrowed a *lot* of money.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:38.800 --> 00:11:43.840
So much, in fact, that paying it all back&nbsp;
was proving to be a bit of a problem.

00:11:44.480 --> 00:11:47.760
Luckily for Philip IV, he was a king.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:47.760 --> 00:11:55.840
And kings have always had their own unique ways of&nbsp;
dealing with problems - usually by murdering them.

00:11:56.400 --> 00:12:00.320
Seizing on the shifting public&nbsp;
sentiment towards the Templars,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:00.320 --> 00:12:06.320
King Philip put together a list of heresies he&nbsp;
claimed were being committed by the secretive&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:06.320 --> 00:12:13.280
Templars in their castles and cathedrals. Those&nbsp;
heresies included spitting and urinating on the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:13.280 --> 00:12:20.800
Cross, worshipping false idols, specifically&nbsp;
Baphomet, and engaging in homosexual activities.

00:12:21.520 --> 00:12:27.600
It was mostly bullshit obviously - well,&nbsp;
apart from that last one. You can't build&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:27.600 --> 00:12:31.680
a secret order of 20,000 men who are&nbsp;
forbidden to fraternise with women&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:31.680 --> 00:12:35.520
without things getting at least&nbsp;
a little bit Brokeback Mountain.

00:12:36.640 --> 00:12:41.120
But King Philip wasn't worried about the&nbsp;
veracity of his claims. All he had to do&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:41.120 --> 00:12:46.080
was find a few prominent Templars willing to&nbsp;
confess to the crimes they were accused of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:46.080 --> 00:12:52.240
and his case would be watertight. And no Templars&nbsp;
were more prominent than Jacques de Molay,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:52.880 --> 00:12:56.720
Grand Master and leader of the entire order.

00:12:57.280 --> 00:13:02.240
You might think it would be difficult to get&nbsp;
the brave Knights Templar to admit to crimes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:02.240 --> 00:13:09.200
they hadn't committed, but remember - this was&nbsp;
a time long before trifling things like human&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:09.200 --> 00:13:14.800
rights existed. If you were a king and you wanted&nbsp;
someone to confess to something they didn't do,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:14.800 --> 00:13:20.000
all you had to do was them torture&nbsp;
them for a bit and you were golden.

00:13:20.000 --> 00:13:24.640
Sure enough, Jacques de Molay - along&nbsp;
with many of his knights - confessed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:24.640 --> 00:13:31.440
to everything. Many would later recant their&nbsp;
confessions once free of the thumbscrews,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:32.000 --> 00:13:37.360
but by then it was too late. The&nbsp;
Templar name had been tarnished, and,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:37.360 --> 00:13:42.800
under pressure from King Philip, the Pope&nbsp;
instructed that every Templar in Europe&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:42.800 --> 00:13:49.200
should be arrested and put on trial. A few&nbsp;
years later, the order was officially disbanded.

00:13:51.120 --> 00:13:54.480
Hundreds, perhaps thousands of&nbsp;
Templars were killed for their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:54.480 --> 00:13:57.920
supposed crimes, including Jacques de Molay,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:57.920 --> 00:14:03.280
who King Philip had burned at the stake on an&nbsp;
island in the middle of the River Seine in Paris.

00:14:04.080 --> 00:14:11.200
It's said that Molay cursed both the Pope and&nbsp;
King Philip even as the flames ate him alive,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:11.840 --> 00:14:16.480
vowing that divine retribution would&nbsp;
be brought down on them both in short&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:16.480 --> 00:14:22.160
order. And it seems the guy knew&nbsp;
how to execute a solid curse,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:22.160 --> 00:14:26.800
because both the Pope and King&nbsp;
Philip were dead within the year.

00:14:27.440 --> 00:14:31.920
Not that it made any difference for the&nbsp;
Templars. Their race was officially run.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:32.640 --> 00:14:38.320
Those that escaped persecution joined other&nbsp;
catholic military orders or simply retired,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:39.040 --> 00:14:41.840
never to raise arms again in the name of God.

00:14:42.800 --> 00:14:47.920
Or at least, that's the official line.&nbsp;
But of course, plenty of people believe&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:47.920 --> 00:14:53.840
the Templars never truly went away. As for&nbsp;
why that is... well, that's a good question,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:54.640 --> 00:14:59.600
and there's no simple answer. In truth, there's&nbsp;
little evidence to support pretty much any of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:59.600 --> 00:15:04.880
many myths and legends that surround the Templars&nbsp;
today. When the organisation was disbanded,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:04.880 --> 00:15:12.000
it left behind a huge void, and over the years&nbsp;
people have filled that void with stories.

00:15:12.000 --> 00:15:16.240
Many of the myths are linked to the mystery&nbsp;
of what happened to the Templar's vast&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:16.240 --> 00:15:21.440
wealth after their demise, but there's actually&nbsp;
a pretty simple answer to that one - King&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:21.440 --> 00:15:27.440
Philip the IV happened to it, appropriating&nbsp;
Templar funds to pay off his mountainous debts.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:28.080 --> 00:15:30.720
Most other European kings did the same.

00:15:31.280 --> 00:15:36.480
These days, many people and organisations&nbsp;
around the world claim to be descendants&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:36.480 --> 00:15:41.600
of the Knights Templar - including the&nbsp;
Freemasons - but almost all of them are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:41.600 --> 00:15:49.040
just appropriating the Templar story because&nbsp;
it sounds cool.The vast majority of so-called&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:49.040 --> 00:15:54.480
'Knights Templar' knocking around today are just&nbsp;
middle-aged men with overactive imaginations&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:54.480 --> 00:15:56.320
who think they look good in chainmail.

00:15:56.880 --> 00:16:02.400
That isn't quite the end of the story though.&nbsp;
Because there is actually one modern organisation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:02.400 --> 00:16:08.880
that genuinely is linked to the Templars - the&nbsp;
Military Order of Christ based in Portugal.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:09.920 --> 00:16:13.760
You see, while the various kings of&nbsp;
Europe were arresting Templars left&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:13.760 --> 00:16:21.840
right and centre by order of the Pope, one&nbsp;
king - Denis of Portugal - refused. Instead,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:21.840 --> 00:16:27.440
he set up the Military Order of Christ and&nbsp;
invited any surviving ex Templars to join.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:29.040 --> 00:16:36.560
The group still exists today more than 700 years&nbsp;
later, but sadly there's no reason to believe&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:36.560 --> 00:16:42.160
they're hiding the Holy Grail, the Ark of the&nbsp;
Covenant, or Jesus and Mary Magdalene's great,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:42.160 --> 00:16:47.520
great, great, great, great, great grandchildren&nbsp;
(sorry to disappoint you Dan Brown).

00:16:47.520 --> 00:16:51.600
The Templar's legacy lives on in other ways&nbsp;
though, too. They did so much building at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:51.600 --> 00:16:55.840
the height of their power that many&nbsp;
Templar landmarks still exist today.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:55.840 --> 00:17:00.160
Temple, the legal district in&nbsp;
London, is named for the Templars,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:00.160 --> 00:17:05.760
along with literally hundreds of churches and&nbsp;
castles throughout Europe and the Middle East.

00:17:05.760 --> 00:17:10.480
The Templars may be gone, at least in their&nbsp;
original form, but they certainly haven’t been&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:10.480 --> 00:17:16.960
forgotten. And if you ask me, the countless myths&nbsp;
and conspiracy theories that surround the order&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:16.960 --> 00:17:21.760
are surplus to requirements - the&nbsp;
actual documented history of these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:21.760 --> 00:17:24.480
warrior monks is better than most of them anyway.

00:17:28.400 --> 00:17:30.880
Thanks for watching.

